- Discuss performance when 128-bit shifts are not optimized by the compiler.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a branchless, correct popcount in O(1) time, a common low-level optimization in performance-critical code (DBs, networking) at senior-level shops like Oracle and Twitter.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit counting
- Parallel bit manipulation with masks
- 32-bit mask progression and final accumulation

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- Compare with hardware POPCNT; what are portability trade-offs?
- How would you test exhaustively or with randomized tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

